/// Tests the owner rules of owner.prover against the oracle, the runs of fir.interpreter and the
/// events of fir.shadow (spec/ownership-design.md 3.6): rule (a), the overwrite check of D17.11,
/// parts 1 and 2 of rule (c), the leak rule of D17.20, part 1 of it for the owning aggregate
/// locals, and rule (b), a use after `move` or `del` of D17.21. The hand cases of the design
/// reviews run as programs, and the seeded family and the shapes of generator.ft run too. The
/// gate judges each item in a closed world: a run of an item gives no error event that no rule
/// of the compiler takes for that item. For each set the test prints the counts of the gate, and
/// the sites, the false accepts and the false rejects of each rule. The typed family of
/// generator.ft has tests of its own in test/fort/owner/typed, with the same judgement.
//
// The gate. Four rules of the compiler refuse an item. The pass gives one of its functions an
// error of D17.11, D17.20 or D17.21, or the drop of a `del(s, d)` of the item is not release-only
// (D17.9). The pass gives one a line of `--leak-report` or a line of `--whole-store-report`
// (take_lines). The item holds a `del` of a span of owners with one operand, which gets a line
// of `--span-del-report` (refuse_dels). An item also has each rule of each item whose function
// it names (refuse_callers). The compiler accepts an item that no rule refuses.
//
// An error refuses the program, so an event of an item with an error gets no ruling: the test
// counts it as `rejected`. Each other event gets its ruling from judgement.ruling_of
// (test/fort/support/judgement.ft), the one judgement that the typed family uses too. It reads
// the one disposition table (test/fort/support/disposition.ft), the cycle mark of the event and
// whether the event stands at a `dead` marker of an abort block. A `leak` there is no error, and
// a `leak` at each other place is one, also when a later statement ends the program. An event of
// a kind that the table trusts is an error: the compiler has no rule for the kind, and an
// accepted program must not give it. A report line takes only an event of its own rule on the
// line that it names: `--leak-report` a `leak`, `--whole-store-report` an `overwrite`, and
// `--span-del-report` a `leak` at its `del`. No line takes a `leak at exit`, a `use after move`
// or an event of a trusted kind. An item fails the gate when a run gives an error event that no
// report line takes. So an accepted item fails for each error event, and an item with a report
// line fails for an error event of another rule or on another line. An item with no error of the
// pass also fails when a run of it ends in a fault other than `null dereference` with no error
// event (judgement.is_silent, judge_end), as a function of the typed family does.
//
// judgement.KNOWN counts the items that fail today by set and by class, and the test stops when
// a set has more or fewer failing items of a class than its row says (hold_known). An item has
// the class of its first error event (judgement.class_of_event), and an item that fails with no
// error event has no class. FLOORS gives the least count of accepted items of
// each family, and the test stops below it (hold_floor). FORCED gives each case of the gate a set
// of report lines, and the test stops when the forced run has another verdict (run_forced). A
// line `gate <kind>` of a set gives the disposition of the kind, its events in items with an
// error (`rejected`), its errors that a report line takes (`excused`), its errors that no line
// takes (`failing`), and its events that are no error.
//
// A `leak at exit` comes from shadow.test_exit, which the interpreter calls only when `main`
// returns (interpreter.run_program). So the gate judges that kind only in a run of a program
// whose `main` returns: in the hand cases and in the seeded family. A run of a shape or of a FIR
// case gives no such event.
//
// The sites. The gate says nothing of an event of an item with an error, so the test also
// holds each rule at its sites: a site that a rule does not reject and where a run gives the event
// of that rule is a false accept, in a refused item too, and it stops the test. A rejected site
// with no event is a false reject, which the test counts. An event marks a site only when the rule
// of that site answers for it, and the paragraphs below say when. An event that marks no site
// changes no count of a site, and the gate judges it as it judges each event.
//
// The hand set holds the cases of the reviews, the item cases, the exit cases, the cases of
// part 2 (DEL_CASES), the cases of rule (b) (USE_CASES), the cases of the owning aggregate locals
// (AGG_CASES), the cases of the gate (GATE_CASES) and the FIR cases.
// Each line of a reject in it comes from the rule, not from a run. fa5 has the one false reject of
// rule (a) of the cases of the reviews: its run calls `bsearch`, which the oracle does not run, so
// no run reaches the store after the call. fa5c calls `oracle_call` in its place, and its store
// has its event. g7 and h7 have the two false rejects of rule (c) of those cases: each frees `x`
// through `*pp`, and the pass relates no place through a pointer to `x`, so the `dead` of `x` at
// the `return` keeps no fact.
//
// An exit site is a function and the location of a `dead` marker of an owner local outside an
// abort block, or of a `return` (design 4.7). The sites come from the FIR, so that a pass that
// lists no obligation at an exit has a site there too. The `dead` markers of one scope share a
// location, so the test gives each its own line first (relocate). The site is rejected when the
// pass does not prove an obligation there. A `leak` event at a `dead` or at a `return` of a local
// owner marks its exit site. A `leak` event at a `return` of a function with a parameter or a
// temporary of an owning aggregate type marks only the site of the owning aggregate locals,
// because it does not say which owner leaked. False accepts and false rejects count as for rule
// (a).
//
// Part 1 of the leak rule on the owning aggregate locals has exit sites of its own
// (add_aggregates): each `dead` of a local of an owning aggregate type outside an abort block, each
// `return` of a function with a parameter or a temporary of such a type, and each exit of
// p->aggregates. The site is rejected when the pass does not prove such an exit there, which makes
// the function an error (has_error), so the test holds the verdict of the error. An exit of an
// owner local that the pass does not prove at the same `return` rejects the site too, because the
// `leak` event at a `return` does not say which owner leaked. Such a site,
// which only the owner local rejects, is masked: a wrong proof of an owning aggregate there gives
// no false accept, so each set counts its masked sites and those of them with an event. Each `leak`
// event marks the site at its location (mark_aggregate). A set prints its line of the owning
// aggregate locals only when it holds such a site.
//
// A site of part 2 is a function and the location of a `del` of a place of type `T* own`, or of a
// `cast` whose operand moves such a place, where `T` holds owners by the checker's mark of its type
// (holds_owners), and not from the target type of the `cast`, which the pass reads. The sites
// come from the FIR, and the obligations of the pass only mark them rejected. A `cast` to a
// pointer to `T` is a site that the pass does not reject, and its object leaks at a `del` of the
// new pointer, a site of its own. A `leak` event at such a `del` marks its site:
// the run reports a `leak` at a `del` that frees an object with an owner slot of a live allocation.
// A `leak` at a `del` of a pointer to an object with no owner by its type comes after a `cast` that
// hid the owners, and it marks the `cast` sites of its function, or of the batch; with no `cast`
// site it is a false accept. A `leak` at a `del` of a span marks no site of part 2: the compiler
// gives the item of such a `del` a line of `--span-del-report` (D17.9), and that line takes the
// event in the gate. A set prints its line of part 2 only when it holds a site of part 2.
//
// A site of rule (b) is a function, the location of an item and a local whose owner the item reads
// (a use of D17.21), from the proof: rejected when the pass does not prove the use, which makes the
// function an error (has_error), so the test holds the verdict of the error. A `use after move`
// event marks the sites of rule (b) when the run of a function F read the vacated slot of a local
// of F: its moved tag names F and the local, and no frame other than that of F read the slot (no
// ` by a callee`). The event does not name the read that gave its null, so it marks each site of
// that function and local: a false accept is such an event with no rejected site of its function
// and local, and a false reject is a rejected site with no such event. An event of a slot of no
// local (a global or an allocation), or that a callee read, marks no site.
//
// A site is a function and the location of a `check(overwrite: p)` or of a store that needs a
// proof, where rule (a) reports its error; the lowering puts the store into `p` at the location of
// its check (spec/fir.md 9.3). The site is rejected when the pass does not prove a check or a store
// there. An `overwrite` event marks a site of rule (a) when it happens at a store into a place of
// an `own` reference type. An `overwrite` event at another store marks none: the rule of the
// whole stores answers for it, with a line of `--whole-store-report` (D17.11). A false accept is a
// site that is not rejected where a run gives such an event, and a false reject is a rejected site
// where no run gives one. Each function of a batch counts, the declarations of
// the batch and std.rt included, for the false accepts. Only the functions of the cases and of
// the families count for the other numbers.
//
// The test keeps only the functions that check, lower and verify (design 3.7): a function whose
// lines hold a diagnostic, or that the lowering or the verifier refuses, leaves its batch, and
// the batch is written and checked again.
import check_env;
import disposition;
import fort.ast;
import fort.check;
import fort.diag;
import fort.fir;
import fort.fir.interpreter;
import fort.fir.release;
import fort.fir.shadow;
import fort.fir_lower;
import fort.fir_parse;
import fort.fir_verify;
import fort.owner.prover;
import fort.owner.report;
import fort.types;
import generator;
import judgement;
import modules_env;
import run_env;
import std.str;
import std.strbuf;

// The count of the functions of a batch of the seeded family and of the shapes.
u64 BATCH = 200;

// The sandbox std/strbuf.ft of the hand cases: the declarations of std.strbuf that they call. An
// `append` grows the buffer before it copies, as std.strbuf does.
u64 STRBUF_LINES = 33;

string[STRBUF_LINES] STRBUF = {
    "struct str_buf {",
    "    u8 mut@ own data;",
    "    u64 len;",
    "}",
    "fn create() str_buf {",
    "    return str_buf{};",
    "}",
    "fn free(str_buf mut* b) void {",
    "    del(b->data);",
    "    b->len = 0;",
    "}",
    "fn reserve(str_buf mut* b, u64 extra) void {",
    "    if (b->len + extra <= b->data.len) {",
    "        return;",
    "    }",
    "    u8 mut@ own bigger = new(u8, 2 * (b->len + extra));",
    "    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < b->len; i++) {",
    "        bigger[i] = b->data[i];",
    "    }",
    "    del(b->data);",
    "    b->data = move(bigger);",
    "}",
    "fn append(str_buf mut* b, string s) void {",
    "    reserve(b, s.len);",
    "    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < s.len; i++) {",
    "        b->data[b->len + i] = cast(s[i], u8);",
    "    }",
    "    b->len += s.len;",
    "}",
    "fn view(str_buf* b) string {",
    "    return cast(b->data[..b->len], string);",
    "}",
    "",
};

// The hand cases of the design reviews (spec/ownership-design.md 5.6), with the k cases of the
// review of R6 and the span cases of the review of this test. Each starts with the line of its
// places (generator.ft), and `$` marks each name of a function. fa5 calls `bsearch`, which the
// oracle does not run, so fa5c calls `oracle_call`. In span_flip and span_local, the store holds
// the address of an element of a span, and the call in its right side changes the span, so the
// store writes the old element, which is live. The cases k2r to k2l bring the address of `x` to a
// store through a pointer by each path of refinement R6 of spec/ownership-design.md 7
// (owner.alias): a call result, a memory local that a callee writes (n4a, the case n4 for rule
// (a)), a global, a place through a pointer, a join and a back edge. In k2o and k2p, R6 keeps the
// fact of `x` past a store through an `own` pointer and through a parameter, and the runs hold
// that it may. k2v stores through a pointer that it loads from a global with no copy into a local.
// The path of an integer that a store puts into memory has no fort source: D3.14 refuses each
// view that lets a store write an integer into a pointer place, as an argument of an `extern fn`
// too. The FIR case `int_store` holds that path.
// The count of the lines of HAND.
u64 HAND_LINES = 673;

string[HAND_LINES] HAND = {
    "// places: arr[i]:i32 arr[j]:i32",
    "fn f$(u64 i, u64 j) i32 {",
    "    i32 mut* own[2] mut arr = {};",
    "    del(arr[i]);",
    "    arr[i] = new(i32);",
    "    *arr[i] = 7;",
    "    i32* v = arr[i];",
    "    del(arr[j]);",
    "    i32 s = *v;",
    "    i32 r = *arr[i];",
    "    del(arr[i]);",
    "    return r + s;",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 { return f$(0, 0); }",
    "// places: x:i32 *pp:i32 *slot:i32",
    "fn put$(i32 mut* own mut* slot) void {",
    "    del(*slot);",
    "    *slot = new(i32);",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    i32 mut* own mut x = new(i32);",
    "    del(x);",
    "    i32 mut* own mut* pp = &x;",
    "    put$(pp);",
    "    x = new(i32);",
    "    del(x);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}",
    "// places: n:i32",
    "struct holder { i32* v; }",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    i32 mut* own n = new(i32);",
    "    *n = 5;",
    "    holder h = holder{n};",
    "    del(n);",
    "    return *h.v;",
    "}",
    "// places: n:i32",
    "fn id$(i32* p) i32* { return p; }",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    i32 mut* own n = new(i32);",
    "    *n = 5;",
    "    i32* v = id$(n);",
    "    del(n);",
    "    return *v;",
    "}",
    "// places: G:i32",
    "extern fn bsearch(void* key, void* base, u64 n, u64 size, fn (void*, void*) i32 cmp) void*;",
    "i32 mut* own mut G = null;",
    "fn cmp$(void* a, void* b) i32 {",
    "    del(G);",
    "    G = new(i32);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    i32 k = 0;",
    "    del(G);",
    "    void* r = bsearch(cast(&k, void*), cast(&k, void*), 1, 4, cmp$);",
    "    G = new(i32);",
    "    del(G);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}",
    "// places: G:i32",
    "import std.libc;",
    "i32 mut* own mut G = null;",
    "fn cmp$(i32 n) i32 {",
    "    del(G);",
    "    G = new(i32);",
    "    return n;",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    del(G);",
    "    i32 r = libc.oracle_call(cmp$, 0);",
    "    G = new(i32);",
    "    del(G);",
    "    return r;",
    "}",
    "// places:",
    "struct holder { i32* p; }",
    "fn f$() holder {",
    "    i32 x = 1;",
    "    holder h = holder{&x};",
    "    return h;",
    "}",
    "fn g$() i32@ {",
    "    i32[4] a = {};",
    "    return a[..];",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 { holder h = f$(); i32@ s = g$(); return *h.p + s[0]; }",
    "// places: items[k]:i32 s[0]:i32",
    "fn refill$(i32 mut* own mut@ s) void {",
    "    del(s[0]);",
    "    s[0] = new(i32);",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
    "    i32 mut* own mut@ own items = new(i32 mut* own, 1);",
    "    u64 k = 0;",
    "    del(items[k]);",
    "    refill$(items);",
    "    items[k] = new(i32);",
    "    del(items[k]);",
    "    del(items);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}",
    "// places: n:i32",
    "struct holder { i32* v; }",
    "fn fill$(holder mut* h) void {",
    "    i32 mut* own n = new(i32);",
    "    *n = 5;",
    "    h->v = n;",
    "    del(n);",
    "}",
    "fn main$() i32 { holder mut h = {}; fill$(&h); return *h.v; }",
    "// places: x:node y:node x->next:node yv->next:node",
    "struct node { node mut* own next; }",
