// The hand cases of part 1 of the leak rule on the owning aggregate locals (D17.20): for each
// exit kind, a function whose exit a run reaches with an owner below a struct local that holds an
// allocation and one whose exit frees it. `brace` leaves a block, `return` returns before its
// `del`s with the parameter `q` live too, `loop` leaves a loop by `break` and by `continue`, and
// `defer` frees at each exit. In `callee` a call that takes the address of the local frees it in
// `free`, a false reject because the pass reads no callee, and keeps it in `leak`. A deferred
// `del` of one line each keeps the seeded family from a store into a deferred block, which expands
// at two exits with one location, so its two checks would join. In `paths`
// the owner holds an allocation on one path only. `array` holds the owners in a fixed array,
// `nested` in a struct and an array of a struct, and `moved` moves the local into a parameter and
// out of a function. In `abort` the block falls off into the block of a `panic`, so its `dead` has
// no obligation. `strbuf` frees a buffer with a call, a false reject, and leaves one. In `masked`
// the owner parameter `p` leaks at the `return` where the pass proves the struct parameter `q`, so
// only `p` rejects the site of `q` there: one masked site with an event. The seeded
// family inserts into these cases too.
// The count of the lines of AGG_CASES.
u64 AGG_LINES = 219;

