// The count of the lines of GATE_CASES.
u64 GATE_LINES = 214;

// The hand cases of the gate, in the format of HAND. The seeded family does not insert into these
// cases. FORCED gives each case a set of rules and names the event that fails it.
//
// In `whole` a store of a whole `duo` goes over a live owner, which the run reports as an
// `overwrite`. The pass gives no error there: the store gets a line of `--whole-store-report`
// (D17.11), and no other line refuses the item. So a gate that does not read that line fails.
// In `before_abort` an owner leaks at the end of an ordinary scope, and a later statement of the
// run ends the program. The compiler gives the error of D17.20, and the `leak` stays an error.
// In `cycle` an allocation owns itself. The compiler accepts the item, and its run gives a `leak
// at exit` of a cycle, which is no error. In `use` a read follows the `move` of a local owner,
// the error of D17.21. In `span` a `del` with one operand frees a span whose element owns an
// allocation, which gets a line of `--span-del-report` (D17.9). In `span_chain` that allocation
// owns a second one, which no `del` frees: the run reports it as a `leak at exit`.
// In `before_check` the scope of the owner ends in a block that ends in a bounds `check`, which
// is no abort block (fir.term_is_abort). The compiler gives the error of D17.20, and the `leak`
// is an error. In `abort_block` the scope ends in the block of a `panic`, an abort block. Its
// exit has no obligation (D17.20): the compiler accepts the item, and the `leak` of its run is no
// error. In `inner` a `del` of a field frees an object whose owner holds an allocation. The
// first owner of the object of the base pointer is empty. The `del` gets a line of
// `--leak-report` (D17.20 part 2).
// The next three cases hold parts of two type tests that the pass reads too. In `nested_array` a
// local is a fixed array of a struct that owns an allocation, an owning aggregate through its
// element (types.type_is_owning_aggregate). Its scope ends with a live owner below it. The
// compiler gives the error of D17.20, and the `leak` of the run is an error. In `unreachable` the
// scope of the owner ends after a loop that no path leaves. Its block ends in `unreachable`, an
// abort block (fir.term_is_abort). That exit has no obligation: the compiler accepts the item,
// and its run gives no event. In `string_owner` a `cast` moves an owning span
// into a `string own`, a reference (types.type_is_reference), and no `del` frees it. The
// compiler gives the error of D17.20, and the `leak` of the run is an error.
// In `whole_chain` the store of `whole` goes over the owner of an allocation that owns a second
// one. The run reports the first as an `overwrite` and the second as a `leak at exit`. The
// compiler gives the item the line of `--whole-store-report` and no other rule, and that line
// does not take the `leak at exit`. So the gate fails the item, and no class of judgement.KNOWN
// holds a `leak at exit`. For that reason the case is no item of the hand set: only FORCED runs
// it (GATE_ITEMS). The rows of FORCED set the lines by hand, so no test holds which lines the
// compiler gives the item.
// In `whole_pointer` the store of a whole `duo` goes through a pointer over a live owner, with a
// line of `--whole-store-report`. In `span_aggregate` a `del` with one operand frees a span of
// structs with an owner, with a line of `--span-del-report`. In `drop_store` the drop of a
// `del(s, d)` stores an owner through an index of a global view of the span, so it is not
// release-only: the item gets the error of D17.9, which only fort.fir.release gives.
// In `vacated_view` a function deletes the owner field of its parameter, copies the vacated
// field into a view and loads through the view. The pass gives no error for the load, and the
// run gives a `use after move` there. The compiler accepts the item, so it fails the gate: it is
// the one failure of the hand set of the class `callee read` in judgement.KNOWN, because `view$`
// reads the vacated slot of the local `s` of `main$` (X2). Its function has the statements of
// the one function that failed in the family of the 97 statements, which this case replaces. The
// operation `callee read` of the typed family reaches the same class.
// In `silent` a function calls itself with no end. The run gives no event and ends in a fault that
// is no `null dereference`, so the item fails the gate with no error event (judge_end), and no
// class of judgement.KNOWN holds it. For that reason only FORCED runs the case.
string[GATE_LINES] GATE_CASES = {
    "// places: s.a:i32",
    "struct duo { i32 mut* own a; i32 mut* own b; }",
    "fn main$() i32 {",
